 "issue": 1707,
 "issue_title": "Bug: restrict write_csv line terminators to newline values",
 "issue_body": "## Summary\n\n`write_csv()` accepts arbitrary non-empty `line_terminator` strings such as `\"END\"` or `\"\\0\"`. This can create files where records are not separated by newline characters, or where NUL bytes are written into the output.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nLine terminators define CSV record boundaries. Allowing arbitrary text makes it easy to generate files that many CSV tools cannot parse, and NUL terminators conflict with Arnio's own binary/corruption checks.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `arnio/io.py`: `write_csv()` validates `line_terminator` is a non-empty string only.\n- Existing validation rejects empty and non-string terminators, but it does not restrict terminators to newline-style values.\n- Runtime behavior writes bytes like `b'aEND1END2END'` when `line_terminator=\"END\"`.\n\n## Reproduction or trigger\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport arnio as ar\n\nframe = ar.from_pandas(pd.DataFrame({\"a\": [1, 2]}))\nar.write_csv(frame, \"out.csv\", line_terminator=\"END\")\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n`write_csv()` should accept only standard CSV line terminators such as `\"\\n\"`, `\"\\r\\n\"`, and possibly `\"\\r\"`, or clearly document broader custom terminators as an advanced escape hatch.\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nAny non-empty string is accepted and written directly between records.\n\n## Suggested direction\n\nAdd explicit validation for `line_terminator` values and tests for invalid arbitrary strings and NUL-containing terminators. Keep valid `\"\\n\"` and `\"\\r\\n\"` behavior unchanged.\n\n## References\n\n- `arnio/io.py`\n- `tests/test_write_csv.py`\n- Related closed issues: #576, #577\r\n",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/1707",
 "pr": 2004,
 "pr_title": "fix: restrict write_csv line_terminator to standard newline values",
